This introduces a new content builder system. The idea is to specify the content compilation from a C# project and run it to actually compile the content.

For this example lets use the Platformer2D from the samples repo as an example. To set up the use of the new content builder system:

- Create a new C# console project
- Reference the `MonoGame.Content.Pipeline` nuget/package
- Reference the respective `MonoGame.Framework` nuget/package
- Add the following code to it.

**Program.cs:**

```csharp
using ContentProject;
using Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Content.Pipeline;
using Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Graphics;
using MonoGame.Framework.Content.Pipeline.Builder;

var contentCollectionArgs = new ContentBuilderParams()
{
    Mode = ContentBuilderMode.Builder,
    WorkingDirectory = "", // path to where your content folder can be located
    SourceDirectory = "Content", // Not actually needed as this is the default, but added for reference
    OutputDirectory = "", // Defaults to "bin/Content" of the Working Directory
    Platform = TargetPlatform.DesktopGL
};
var contentCollector = new MyContentCollector();
contentCollector.Run(contentCollectionArgs); // alternativly just pass args to read from command line
```

**MyCollector.cs:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Content.Pipeline;
using MonoGame.Framework.Content.Pipeline.Builder;

namespace ContentProject;

public class MyContentCollector : ContentBuilder
{
    public override IContentCollection GetContentCollection()
    {
        var contentCollection = new ContentCollection();
        
        // include everything in the folder
        contentCollection.Include<RegexRule>(".");

        // override .txt files to be copied
        contentCollection.IncludeCopy<RegexRule>(".txt");

        // exclude bin / obj paths
        contentCollection.Exclude<RegexRule>("bin/");
        contentCollection.Exclude<RegexRule>("obj/");
        
        return contentCollection;
    }
}

```

Afterwards run from the working directory with `Content` in it or simply override the `WorkingDirectory` and `SourceDirectory`. The content for Platformer2D should now get compiled.

This was a quick example of trying to use the system to build content. Now with that out of the way lets talk about the features the system offers:

- The content builder can be run in 2 modes, builder mode and server mode:
  - **Builder** mode is used for building all the content for the specified platform before shipping.
  - **Server** mode is used for development purposes, its goal is to allow developers to compile content as its needed instead of compiling everything ahead of time each time they compile code.
    - Later on the idea would be to upgrade this mode to allow hot reload of assets as well.
    - Right now this provides with a `ContentServer` class which can be inherited from to provide your own implementations for the server (needed for private platforms as some don't get connected to the network).
    - When launching server mode, it will launch all the available `ContentServer` types.
    - I've setup the `NetworkContentServer` class using an HTTPClient for both client and server side but this will need to be reworked when we get to hot reload.
    - To use it simply assign the `TitleContainer.ContentProvider` static property to an apropriate `IContentProvider`, ie:
        ```cs
        TitleContainer.ContentProvider = new NetworkContentProvider()
        {
            Address = "http://127.0.0.1:7771/"
        };
        ```
    - In order to avoid weird dependencies or shipping debug code, I've added a new classlib called `MonoGame.Framework.DebugAdapters` that contains the `NetworkContentProvider` class. Right now it only contains it but it can be a place to put in stuff that should not be part of MonoGame assembly that we wish to provide for debugging purposes, *caugh* imgui *caugh*.
- `ContentBuilderParams` specify the platform information for which the `ContentBuilder` should run for
  - For convinience this can also be specified by passing the cli `args` variable so that you can run the content builder with different platform parameters easilly
- The content collection for both mode is done by a `CollectContent` method as seen above by filling up a collection of type `IContentCollection`.
  - We have a special implementation for filling up this collection as its purpose is not to hold all the current filepaths but take into account any new filepaths or changes that might happen while the **Server** mode is running (see sample code above).
    - Our internal implementation is called `ContentCollection` and uses `ContentRule`s for figuring out what status the content should be compiled with by appending each rule to a list, and then passing the contents filepath through each of the rules.
    - There are 2 sets of rules for including and exluding content that we provide by default, those are `RegexRule` and `WildcardRule`
    - `WildcardRule` uses Visual Basics `Like` operator for its implementation as C# has no official one and we want consistency between platforms.
    - A custom rule can be setup by making a child class of `ContentRule`.
  - We could make an `MGCBCollection` later on that just reads all the content information from an .mgcb file to use with the new system.
- The content caching exists and is being done by `IContentCache` implementation that can be specified by the `ContentBuilder.ContentCache` property.
  - By default its enabled and set to use an internal implementation.
  - Right now the internal implementation does not track code changes of importers / processors, I'll open up an issue on how we wanna handle this as I don't like just checking for date time of assemblies as we do for the mgcb.
- Misc:
  - docs - I've documented all the publicly exposed code alredy.
  - tests - API is still in development so I thought it better to let it evolve first before adding any.
  - templates - Separate repo, I'll add some once this is merged.
